<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Campus Brain — dark editorial palette.

  The app is used one-handed, indoors, often in a badly lit corridor between
  classes, and its whole promise is that an answer is trustworthy. So: a near
  black ground that stays readable at low brightness, one warm accent that
  nothing else in the interface is allowed to borrow, and route colours that
  read as meaning rather than decoration.

  The accent is amber, not blue. Every model-backed product on a student's
  phone is blue or violet; amber reads as a highlighter over a record, which
  is what this app actually does.
-->
<resources>

    <!-- Ground. Four steps, each one a real surface, not a gradient. -->
    <color name="ink">#0A0C10</color>          <!-- app background -->
    <color name="ink_raised">#12151B</color>   <!-- cards, answer surface -->
    <color name="ink_input">#171B22</color>    <!-- fields, pressed states -->
    <color name="hairline">#242A34</color>     <!-- 1dp separators, borders -->

    <!--
      Type. Secondary is tinted from the ground's hue, never neutral grey, so
      it sits in the same world instead of looking washed out.

      Ratios are quoted against ink_input, the darkest-contrast case, because
      that is the surface the input hints sit on and a ramp is only as good as
      its worst ground. The figures here were previously quoted "on ink" but
      measured on ink_raised, which flattered them.

      text_tertiary was #68717F, annotated "4.6:1 on ink, meets AA". It
      measured 3.97 on ink, 3.71 on ink_raised and 3.50 on ink_input, so it
      missed AA on every surface — and it carries nineteen references,
      including every unselected nav label, the search and question hints, and
      all of the answer card's metadata. None of them are near the 24sp the
      large-text exemption needs.

      #828C9E clears 4.5:1 on all three grounds with about half a point of
      headroom, so a later tweak to a surface colour cannot silently push it
      back under. The smallest passing lift, #7C8696, cleared ink_input by
      only 0.19 and was rejected for that reason.

      Known cost, and it needs a look on a real device: raising the floor
      compresses the ramp. Secondary against tertiary is now 1.32:1 where
      primary against secondary is 2.38:1, so the bottom two tiers are a much
      softer step than the top two. If they do not read as distinct in a
      corridor, the honest fix is to collapse to two tiers rather than keep a
      distinction nobody can see — not to darken tertiary back below AA.
    -->
    <color name="text_primary">#F4F6FA</color>    <!-- 15.96:1 on ink_input -->
    <color name="text_secondary">#98A2B3</color>  <!--  6.70:1 on ink_input -->
    <color name="text_tertiary">#828C9E</color>   <!--  5.09:1 on ink_input, AA -->

    <!-- The single accent. Reserved for the answer and the primary action. -->
    <color name="accent">#FFB224</color>
    <color name="accent_dim">#8A5F12</color>
    <color name="on_accent">#150E00</color>

    <!-- Route identity. Desaturated to live on near-black; each one names how
         the answer was reached, so they must stay distinguishable at 10sp. -->
    <color name="route_tabular">#3FCF8E</color>  <!-- computed by SQL -->
    <color name="route_fact">#6AA8FF</color>     <!-- quoted from a document -->
    <color name="route_local">#C48BFF</color>    <!-- followed a relationship -->
    <color name="route_global">#4ECDC4</color>   <!-- read across the corpus -->

    <!-- Verdicts. -->
    <color name="verified">#3FCF8E</color>
    <color name="grade_pass">#3FCF8E</color>
    <color name="grade_fail">#F1656B</color>
    <!--
      There is no grade_audit fill any more, and that is the point.

      It was #68717F, which put near-black ink on it at 3.9:1 and missed AA.
      Lifting it to #808A99 fixed the ratio and then collided with the raised
      text_tertiary (#828C9E) — a badge whose fill matches the text beside it
      reads as a bug, not as a tier.

      An audit is not a verdict, so it should never have been wearing a
      verdict's filled badge. ResultFragment draws it outlined instead, on
      bg_chip in the text ramp: present, counted, not scored. Pass and fail
      keep the two saturated fills, which is now the only thing a fill means
      on this screen.
    -->

    <!-- Retained: the launcher background is referenced by the mipmap. -->
    <color name="brand">#0A0C10</color>
    <color name="on_brand">#F4F6FA</color>
</resources>
